                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Unfortunately, while I do like the core concept, the execution feels extremely barren and lacking.\nThe gameplay itself is rough and basic, but I did find it somewhat enjoyable.\nBut there are:\n- No accessibility or control options \n- No keybind remapping\n- No sensitivity slider\nFor a game whose entire gameplay revolves around precise movement, adding at least some, or at least one, of these options would be the bare minimum options.\nAlso there simply isn't much game here. \n- Only 5 levels,that can be cleared relatively quickly.\nIt wouldn't necessarily be a dealbreaker on its own, especially since the game seems to be positioning itself more as a language-learning experience than an arcadey game. However, even in that regard it falls short, leaving a lot to be desired.\n\nThe language-learning aspect is more disappointing.\nRussian has a comparatively substantial amount of content, but the other supported languages have such an insignificant number of phrases and entries that they feel almost like placeholders. I would honestly have preferred the developer to focus entirely on Russian and make that experience complete rather than add several other languages just for show with only a laughably small amount of material.\n- Russian 1033 entries: (50 basic phrases + 866 Proverbs & sayings + 117 Irregular verb quizzes) \nNo entries to study Cyrillic Alphabet, the Cyrillic can be inferred from the other entries, Even though the cyrillic alphabet it self can be learn easely externaly, its strage that a proper module to learn it missing since I suppose this is targeted at total and immidiate beginners, or basic A1 knowledge \n(A similar issue is present whit Japanese)\n- French 72 entries: (37 basic phrases + 35 Irregular verb quizzes)\n- German : 133 basic phrases\n- Spanish : 53 basic phrases \n- Japanese : 45 basic phrases + (112 katakana's 拗音 (yōon))\n(No hirgana's yōon, most kana and some compounds can be inferred from the basic phrases)\n- Chinese : 22 basic phrases\nMore importantly, the game doesn't provide the tools necessary to actually learn from the vocabulary it presents.\nThere are no framework for actually learning, reviewing, and retaining it:\n- No proper Flashcards, \n- No memory tables, \n- No vocabulary or quizzes archives\n- Nor any other way to review previously encountered words and phrases.\nThere is no meaningful system for tracking what you've already seen, what you've answered correctly or incorrectly, or what you need to practice again. If you actually want to retain the vocabulary, you're basically left writing it down on paper or transferring it to external software yourself.\nA particularly grave omission, given that repetition and review are instrumental to vocabulary acquisition. If the game is going to present itself as a language-learning experience, being this just a notionist mnemonic part of language learning and not integrating the core aspects of it, I would expect at the very least to be able to return to words and phrases already encountered to practice them again.\n\nAnd that's ultimately the biggest problem with Approaching Blocks, it seems to want to be both a game and a language-learning tool, but doesn't develop either side even remotely enough.\n\nSince I got it for free I dont mind, but I would not recommend this 2€ \"game\" even to beginner russian learners, since there are way better and useful gamified and not experiences, that are even free or around the same price.",
